WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.048
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:16.714
Unit twenty-six: Remote and Hybrid Teams. Today you will project ongoing duration with the future perfect continuous.

4
00:00:17.014 --> 00:00:19.597
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.080
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.034
Remote work.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.710
Remote work means working from a location outside the main office.

8
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:34.187
Remote work became common after 2020.

9
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:37.447
Your turn. Say: Remote work.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.406
Remote work.

11
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.023
Hybrid.

12
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:46.724
Hybrid means combining office and remote work.

13
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:50.303
Our hybrid model requires two office days a week.

14
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.581
Your turn. Say: Hybrid.

15
00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:57.272
Hybrid.

16
00:00:58.017 --> 00:00:59.145
Collaboration.

17
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.670
Collaboration means working together to achieve shared goals.

18
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:07.014
Online whiteboards made collaboration easier.

19
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:10.234
Your turn. Say: Collaboration.

20
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.426
Collaboration.

21
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:16.150
Time zone.

22
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.125
Time zone means a region with the same standard time.

23
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:23.970
Working across five time zones complicates meetings.

24
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.957
Your turn. Say: Time zone.

25
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.789
Time zone.

26
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.830
Asynchronous.

27
00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:36.536
Asynchronous means not happening at the same time.

28
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.939
Asynchronous updates replaced many meetings.

29
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.191
Your turn. Say: Asynchronous.

30
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.301
Asynchronous.

31
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:48.772
Trust.

32
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:52.921
Trust means confidence that others will act reliably.

33
00:01:53.517 --> 00:01:56.364
Remote teams run on trust rather than supervision.

34
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.463
Your turn. Say: Trust.

35
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.046
Trust.

36
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:04.633
Tools.

37
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.474
Tools means software and devices used for work.

38
00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:11.570
The right tools reduce communication friction.

39
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:14.511
Your turn. Say: Tools.

40
00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:18.368
Tools.

41
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:19.990
Wellbeing.

42
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:23.842
Wellbeing means a state of physical and mental health.

43
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.251
Isolation can harm remote workers' wellbeing.

44
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.409
Your turn. Say: Wellbeing.

45
00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:34.268
Wellbeing.

46
00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:39.205
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:45.976
Visal, your software team has staff in Phnom Penh, Hanoi and Berlin. How long has it been remote?

48
00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:54.899
By next March, we will have been working remotely for five years, so we've learned a lot through trial and error.

49
00:02:55.983 --> 00:02:57.693
What's been the biggest challenge?

50
00:02:58.783 --> 00:03:08.228
Time zones. We rely on asynchronous communication: clear written updates, so nobody waits for a meeting to make progress.

51
00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:11.757
And how do you maintain team spirit?

52
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:22.840
Deliberately. By the end of the year, we'll have been running virtual coffee chats every Friday for three years, and they matter more than you'd think.

53
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.349
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:29.634
Grammar focus: Future perfect continuous.

55
00:03:29.934 --> 00:03:31.016
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.717 --> 00:03:34.912
By then, we will have been working remotely for five years.

57
00:03:35.817 --> 00:03:39.802
By the end of the year, we'll have been running virtual chats for three years.

58
00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:43.842
By June, she will have been leading the team for a decade.

59
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:56.334
Use 'will have been + -ing' to emphasise how long an activity will have continued up to a future point, usually with 'for + duration'. Avoid it with state verbs: 'will have known' (not 'will have been knowing').

60
00:03:57.083 --> 00:04:00.792
Be careful! Don't say: We will have been work for years.

61
00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:05.488
Say: We will have been working for years.

62
00:04:05.788 --> 00:04:07.195
Will have been + -ing.

63
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.664
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:13.025
Leading From a Distance.

65
00:04:13.325 --> 00:04:20.352
The global shift to remote work, accelerated by the pandemic, has permanently changed expectations about where and how work is done.

66
00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:29.345
Hybrid arrangements offer flexibility and access to wider talent pools, but they also create risks of isolation and unequal visibility.

67
00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:37.732
Effective remote leaders favour asynchronous communication, documenting decisions so that colleagues in different time zones can contribute.

68
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:46.108
They also measure results rather than hours online, since constant monitoring undermines the trust on which distributed teams depend.

69
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:54.243
By the end of this decade, many organisations will have been operating in hybrid mode for years, making these leadership skills essential.

70
00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:59.219
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:03.348
By 2030, I blank English for twenty years.

72
00:05:03.648 --> 00:05:07.743
A, will have been learning. B, will be learnt. C, have been learning.

73
00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.300
The answer is A: will have been learning.

74
00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.809
Not happening at the same time is blank.

75
00:05:17.109 --> 00:05:20.844
A, hybrid. B, asynchronous. C, remote.

76
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.992
The answer is B: asynchronous.

77
00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.927
A state of physical and mental health is blank.

78
00:05:31.227 --> 00:05:34.800
A, collaboration. B, trust. C, wellbeing.

79
00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:40.635
The answer is C: wellbeing.

80
00:05:41.783 --> 00:05:43.562
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.862 --> 00:05:51.405
A, By May, he will have been knowing her a year.. B, By May, he will have known her for a year.. C, By May, he will been knowing her..

82
00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:58.766
The answer is B: By May, he will have known her for a year..

83
00:05:59.917 --> 00:06:02.764
According to the reading, effective remote leaders measure…

84
00:06:03.064 --> 00:06:07.356
A, hours online. B, results. C, keyboard activity.

85
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.074
The answer is B: results.

86
00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:17.383
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.609
What are the challenges of leading a remote team?

88
00:06:37.417 --> 00:06:38.998
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.298 --> 00:07:17.102
Leading a remote team is challenging because many informal signals disappear. A manager cannot easily notice when someone is struggling, isolated or confused, so problems may grow unnoticed. Communication across time zones also requires discipline, especially clear written updates and well-documented decisions. Another challenge is building trust without constant supervision. Once a team has been working remotely for a year, its culture should rely on results, not visibility.

90
00:07:17.402 --> 00:07:19.343
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.934
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future perfect continuous.

92
00:07:25.234 --> 00:07:30.394
Today's value is collaboration. Work together, and share both the effort and the success.

93
00:07:30.694 --> 00:07:34.043
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:36.752
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

